<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="extended VTC2 cycle" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY4FS-13" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The product of the |FRAME: AT4G26850| gene (named for Vitamin C defficiency) has been originally classified as GDP-L-galactose phosphorylase. However, the term was misleading for several reasons. First, the enzyme can accept |FRAME: GDP-D-GLUCOSE| just as efficiently as |FRAME: GDP-L-GALACTOSE|. Secondly, multiple sugar 1-phosphates, such as |FRAME: MANNOSE-1P|, |FRAME: GLC-1-P|, and |FRAME: GALACTOSE-1P|, are actually better guanylyl acceptors than phosphate. Thus the enzyme is better described as |FRAME: EC-2.7.7.69| |CITS: [17485667][17877701]|. When acting on |FRAME: GDP-L-GALACTOSE|, the enzyme&apos;s activity leads to formation of |FRAME: CPDQT-4|, which is intermediate in the main |FRAME: ASCORBATE| biosynthesis pathway in plants (see |FRAME: PWY-882|). The gualylyltransferase activity allows the enzyme to utilize |FRAME: GLC-1-P| and |FRAME: MANNOSE-1P| as phosphate sources. |FRAME: ASCORBATE| biosynthesis usually starts with |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE|, which can be produced from |FRAME: D-glucopyranose-6-phosphate| in several steps as described in |FRAME: PWY-5659|. However, due to the |FRAME: AT4G26850| enzyme |FRAME: CPDQT-4| can be formed in different ways using the so called |FRAME: PWY4FS-12| and |FRAME: PWY4FS-13|, bypassing the need for |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE| production from |FRAME: D-glucopyranose-6-phosphate|. The importance of VTC2 is clear since Arabidopsis double mutants of |FRAME: AT4G26850|/|FRAME: AT5G55120| are seedling lethal (they can be rescued by supplementation with L-galactose; |FRAME: AT5G55120| is a minor form of the enzyme with similar activity but considerably lower expression) |CITS:[17877701]|. The VTC2 cycle incorporates two enzymes - |FRAME: EC-2.7.7.69| (VTC2) and |FRAME: EC-5.1.3.18| (GME). GME can restore |FRAME: GDP-L-GALACTOSE| from |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE|, thus enabling VTC2 to continue to produce |FRAME: CPDQT-4| |CITS: [17485667]|. The |FRAME: PWY4FS-13| takes into account the activity of VTC2 with the very abundant |FRAME: GLC-1-P| (derived from photosynthesis) as the guanylyl acceptor, driving production of |FRAME: CPDQT-4| and thus L-ascorbate production. The product of such a reaction, |FRAME: GDP-D-GLUCOSE|, strongly inhibits |FRAME: EC-5.1.3.18|. Thus the authors speculated that another enzyme, a putative GDP-D-mannose 2&quot;-epimerase, catalyzes its conversion to |FRAME: GDP-MANNOSE|. The conversion of the &quot;toxic&quot; |FRAME: GDP-D-GLUCOSE| by the extended VTC2 cycle makes the extended pathway more favorable than the original |FRAME: PWY4FS-12| (reviewed in |CITS:[17950389]|).</Description>
